  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nTODAY AND TONIGHT, early morning satellite imagery showed some \nhigh clouds moving, while at the surface high pressure remained \nin control leading to light winds with temperatures in the teens\nto single digits. \n\nFurther upstream, a potent shortwave trough was located across\nsouthern Alberta, and this will drift towards the Upper Midwest\nlater today. As it does, low level theta_e advection will\noverspread the Upper Minnesota River Valley, with clouds and\nprecipitation developing over southwest Minnesota this evening \nand overnight. Forecast soundings show temperatures below \nfreezing along with some, but not a lot, of lift. However, the \nforcing should be enough to produce several hours of light \nprecipitation, which will fall in the form of snow. For that \nreason, did increase precipitation chances to roughly 80 percent\nfor locations along the upper Minnesota River Valley, as nearly\nall of the guidance indicates at least some snow. \n\nThe upper level wave will pivot overhead, which could enhance\nthe lift, giving some locations close to an inch of snow. Ground\ntemperatures are below freezing given the recent stretch of\ncooler weather, and today's high temperature will only be in the\nmid 30s, so expect the snow to accumulate on grassy and\nuntreated surfaces as it starts to fall shortly after sunset.\n\nThis will lead to slick travel conditions Tuesday night into\nWednesday morning where the heaviest snow falls. Road \nconditions will improve on Wednesday as temperatures warm back \ninto the mid to upper 30s.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY, expect quiet and seasonable weather\nwith no travel concerns during the Thanksgiving Holiday. The\nonly chance for some light rain/snow is with a weak frontal\nboundary Friday night into early Saturday across western \nWisconsin, but this shouldn't amount to anything of \nconsequence. Worst case scenario would be a dusting to a half \ninch of snow with sub-freezing road temperatures. The GEFS 24.00\nensemble has only 1 of 30 members showing over an inch of snow,\nand the ECMWF ensemble doesn't have any for Eau Claire WI, so \nconfidence is high that this will be a null event.\n\nOtherwise the Upper Midwest remains split between the storm\ntrack to the south, and storm track to the north, which means\ndry conditions with a mix of sun and clouds and temperatures\nnear to slightly above the seasonal average for late November.",
